# Figma MCP to WordPress Implementation Instructions

Use this rule when implementing the PHYTO.bg catalog site from Figma through the Figma MCP server in Cursor.

This file should be used together with:

- `wordpress-acf-theme-instructions-updated.mdc`
- `wordpress-acf-theme-skill-updated.mdc`
- `selecta-phyto-catalog-project-context.mdc`
- `selecta-phyto-sitemap-context.mdc`

The Figma design is the main source of truth for visual UI implementation. The WordPress/ACF/CPT rules are the source of truth for architecture and content modeling. The PHYTO project context is the source of truth for scope.

## Critical project scope

The site is a catalog/information WordPress site, not an e-commerce site.

When Figma contains e-commerce-like elements copied from the reference site, do not implement them as commerce functionality.

Do not implement:

- WooCommerce.
- Shopify inside WordPress.
- Buy buttons.
- Add-to-cart.
- Cart.
- Checkout.
- Price/payment/shipping/order logic.
- Stock/inventory logic.
- Customer login.
- Registration.
- Account pages.
- Wishlist.
- Order history.
- Customer dashboard.
- Public user/auth flows.
- Quiz module.

WordPress admin users are allowed for content management. The restriction is against public/customer auth and commerce features.

## What the Figma MCP is for

Use the Figma MCP to inspect structured design context:

- Selected frames.
- Components.
- Component properties and variants.
- Layer names.
- Layout structure.
- Auto Layout intent.
- Spacing.
- Typography.
- Colors.
- Variables/tokens.
- Image assets.
- Icons.
- Responsive variants if present.
- Design annotations and dev resources if present.

Do not treat MCP output as final production code. Adapt it to this WordPress theme using PHP templates, ACF fields, CPTs, and plain CSS.

## Required workflow before coding from Figma

When the user provides a Figma link or asks to implement a design:

1. Identify the exact frame/component/page to implement.
2. Use Figma MCP to fetch design context for the smallest useful selected frame, not the whole file.
3. Summarize what the frame represents:
   - page type
   - section/component name
   - desktop/mobile state
   - data/content requirements
   - reusable components involved
4. Map the design to WordPress architecture:
   - template file
   - template part/component file
   - ACF fields
   - CPT/taxonomy data if relevant
   - CSS component block
   - JS only if required
5. Compare the Figma design against project scope:
   - remove commerce elements
   - remove account/auth elements
   - keep catalog/editorial UI
6. Implement the smallest coherent piece.
7. Verify markup, CSS, escaping, and responsiveness.
8. Avoid unrelated refactors.

## Preferred Figma selection strategy

Always prefer small selections.

Good selections:

```text
Single button component
Product card component
Header desktop frame
Header mobile frame
Mega menu frame
Homepage hero section
Product grid section
Product detail hero section
Ingredients section
FAQ accordion section
Footer section
One complete page frame only when needed for page-level spacing/order
```

Avoid selecting:

```text
Entire huge file
All pages at once
All desktop + mobile frames at once
Deeply nested unorganized groups
Unrelated flows
Very large page frames when only one section is needed
```

If MCP is slow, incomplete, or confused, retry with a smaller frame or component.

## Figma file expectations

The agent should get better output when the Figma file uses:

- Components for repeated UI such as buttons, cards, nav items, product cards, accordions, and newsletter forms.
- Variables for color, spacing, radius, and typography.
- Semantic layer names such as `ProductCard`, `ProductImage`, `HeaderNav`, `MegaMenu`, `HeroTitle`, not `Group 12`.
- Auto Layout for responsive layout intent.
- Separate desktop and mobile frames when the design changes meaningfully.
- Annotations for behavior that cannot be inferred visually.
- Dev resources for implementation notes where available.

If the Figma file is messy, still implement the design, but infer cautiously and prefer clean WordPress/CSS structure over copying bad layer structure.

## Design-to-code mapping

Map Figma objects to WordPress theme concepts like this:

```text
Figma page frame
  -> WordPress template or page with ACF flexible sections

Figma repeated section
  -> template part in template-parts/components/

Figma product card
  -> template-parts/components/product-card.php

Figma advice/article card
  -> template-parts/components/advice-card.php

Figma header/nav
  -> header.php + header component template parts

Figma mega menu
  -> WordPress nav menu + optional ACF menu metadata + template part

Figma footer
  -> footer.php + footer component template part

Figma product listing page
  -> archive-selecta_product.php or taxonomy-selecta_*.php

Figma product detail page
  -> single-selecta_product.php

Figma blog/advice page
  -> archive-selecta_advice.php or single-selecta_advice.php

Figma legal/brand/contact page
  -> WordPress page template or default page with flexible sections
```

Do not create static hardcoded pages when the content should be editable.

## Template and component rules

For each Figma section, decide whether it is:

1. Global reusable component.
2. Page-specific component.
3. CPT-driven listing.
4. CPT-driven single template section.
5. Flexible ACF layout.
6. Static legal/content page markup.

Implementation preference:

```text
Reusable UI
  -> template-parts/components/{component-name}.php

Product card/grid
  -> CPT query + reusable product-card component

Homepage/content sections
  -> ACF flexible content layouts

Product detail content
  -> selecta_product CPT fields + template parts

Advice/editorial
  -> selecta_advice CPT + card/single templates

Header/footer/global values
  -> ACF Options fields
```

## ACF mapping rules from Figma

Use ACF when the design shows content editors should control.

Examples:

```text
Hero title/text/image/CTA
  -> ACF fields or flexible layout fields

Product specification content
  -> Product CPT ACF fields

Product ingredients section
  -> Product CPT repeater/flexible fields

Product benefit cards
  -> Product CPT repeater fields

Homepage product carousel
  -> relationship field or taxonomy query selector

Promotion/banner cards
  -> flexible content layout or selecta_promotion CPT

Brand story section
  -> page flexible fields or options fields

FAQ accordion
  -> FAQ CPT, repeater field, or relationship field depending on reuse

Newsletter block
  -> options fields for copy + integration/embed config
```

Do not hardcode Bulgarian copy unless it is a fixed label. Use CMS fields for marketing/content copy.

## CSS extraction rules

When using Figma values:

- Convert Figma colors to CSS custom properties where they repeat.
- Convert Figma spacing to a small spacing scale when practical.
- Convert typography to reusable CSS variables/classes.
- Use plain CSS.
- Use class selectors.
- Keep specificity low.
- Mobile-first.
- Use `clamp()` for fluid typography/spacing when appropriate.
- Use CSS Grid/Flexbox rather than absolute positioning unless the design truly requires overlay positioning.
- Preserve focus styles and accessible states even if not visible in Figma.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.

Do not blindly copy pixel-perfect absolute positions from Figma if it would break responsiveness.

Suggested token extraction:

```css
:root {
	--color-brand-primary: #111;
	--color-brand-accent: #7a5b43;
	--color-surface: #f6f1eb;
	--color-text: #111;
	--color-muted: #666;

	--font-base: system-ui, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", sans-serif;

	--space-xs: 0.5rem;
	--space-sm: 1rem;
	--space-md: 1.5rem;
	--space-lg: 2rem;
	--space-xl: 4rem;

	--radius-sm: 0.25rem;
	--radius-md: 0.75rem;
}
```

## Asset handling rules

For images and icons from Figma:

- Prefer real WordPress media fields for content images.
- Use Figma-exported assets only for fixed decorative assets, icons, or temporary implementation placeholders.
- Do not hardcode product images from Figma into product templates. Product images should come from the `selecta_product` featured image/gallery.
- Use SVG inline only when safe and simple, or as an asset file if reused.
- Optimize exported image sizes.
- Use `wp_get_attachment_image()` for WordPress-managed images.
- Add meaningful alt text through WordPress media or ACF fields when needed.
- Treat decorative images as decorative.

## Product card rules from Figma

When implementing a Figma product card:

Keep:

- Image.
- Product title.
- Product line/range.
- Short benefit/claim.
- Badge such as "new" or non-commerce label.
- Link to catalog product detail page.

Remove or ignore:

- Price.
- Discount.
- Sale price.
- Add-to-cart button.
- Buy button.
- Rating stars if they require public review flow.
- Stock/shipping/payment text.
- Wishlist/account actions.

Neutral CTA examples:

```text
Виж продукта
Научи повече
Разгледай
```

Only include a CTA if the Figma design requires one and it is non-commerce.

## Product detail rules from Figma

When implementing a Figma product detail page:

Keep:

- Product title.
- Product line.
- Subtitle/claim.
- Image gallery.
- Size/format if catalog-relevant.
- Formula claims.
- Description.
- Key ingredients.
- Full ingredient list.
- Benefits/results.
- Usage instructions.
- Related products.
- Related advice.

Remove or ignore:

- Price.
- Add-to-cart.
- Quantity selector.
- Payment.
- Shipping.
- Loyalty points.
- Customer review submission.
- Login/account prompt.
- Wishlist.
- Purchase incentives.

## Header and navigation rules from Figma

When implementing the Figma header:

Keep:

- Logo.
- Main navigation.
- Mega menu.
- Mobile menu.
- Search if it is catalog/content search.
- Announcement bar if present.
- Store locator/partners link if needed.

Remove or ignore:

- Account icon/link.
- Cart icon/link.
- Order links.
- Customer login/register.
- Country selector unless the Bulgarian site needs it.

If the design includes account/cart because it was copied from the French reference, do not implement them.

## Forms from Figma

Allowed forms:

- Newsletter signup.
- Contact form.
- Search form.
- Store locator filter form if needed.

Not allowed:

- Login form.
- Register form.
- Checkout form.
- Customer account form.
- Wishlist/account preferences.

Form implementation rules:

- Use a trusted WordPress plugin if selected by the project.
- For custom forms, use nonces, validation, sanitization, escaping, and spam protection.
- Keep consent/GDPR copy visible where required.
- Newsletter signup is not customer account creation.

## JavaScript rules

Use JavaScript only when needed for interaction:

- Mobile menu toggle.
- Mega menu interaction.
- Accordions.
- Sliders/carousels only if the design truly requires them.
- Product filter UI if it cannot be handled by links/forms.
- Search overlay if designed.

Rules:

- Prefer simple vanilla JS.
- Do not introduce React/Vue/Svelte.
- Do not add large libraries for simple toggles.
- Ensure keyboard accessibility.
- Sync ARIA states such as `aria-expanded`.
- Respect reduced motion.
- Do not put core content behind JavaScript.

## Responsive implementation

When Figma includes desktop and mobile frames:

1. Compare layout differences.
2. Build mobile-first CSS.
3. Add media queries only at real layout breakpoints.
4. Keep content order logical in the DOM.
5. Do not duplicate entire markup for desktop/mobile unless necessary.
6. Ensure the mobile menu and mega menu are keyboard usable.

When Figma only includes desktop:

- Infer mobile behavior using existing project patterns.
- Keep product grids, cards, and sections responsive.
- Avoid fixed widths that break smaller screens.

## Accessibility requirements

Even if the Figma design omits accessibility details, implement them:

- Semantic landmarks.
- Logical heading order.
- One clear page H1.
- Alt text for meaningful images.
- Real buttons for actions.
- Real links for navigation.
- Visible focus states.
- Keyboard-operable menus and accordions.
- Labels for forms.
- No color-only meaning.
- Sufficient contrast where possible.
- `aria-expanded` and `aria-controls` for toggles.

## Implementation prompt pattern for Cursor

When using Cursor with Figma MCP, use prompts like:

```text
Use the Figma MCP to inspect this selected frame: [Figma frame link].
Implement it as a WordPress classic-theme component for this project.

Constraints:
- Use PHP template parts and plain CSS.
- Use ACF fields where content should be editable.
- Products are selecta_product CPT catalog entries.
- No WooCommerce, no cart, no checkout, no buy button, no price, no public auth.
- Match the design visually but keep semantic HTML and accessible behavior.
- Tell me which files you will edit before editing.
```

For a product card:

```text
Use the Figma MCP to inspect this product card component: [Figma link].
Implement it as template-parts/components/product-card.php plus CSS.

Data source:
- selecta_product CPT
- featured image
- title
- product_line taxonomy or ACF field
- short_description / short claim

Remove:
- price
- rating submission
- add-to-cart
- wishlist
```

For a full page:

```text
Use the Figma MCP to inspect this page frame: [Figma link].
First break it into reusable WordPress components and ACF flexible sections.
Do not code the whole page in one static file.
Then implement the first section/component only.
```

## File-editing discipline

Before editing files, the agent should state:

```text
Design source:
WordPress mapping:
Files to edit:
ACF/CPT data needed:
Commerce/auth elements removed:
```

Example:

```text
Design source: Figma Product Card component
WordPress mapping: reusable product card for selecta_product loops
Files to edit:
  - template-parts/components/product-card.php
  - assets/css/main.css
ACF/CPT data needed:
  - featured image
  - title
  - product_subtitle or short_description
  - product_line taxonomy
Commerce/auth elements removed:
  - price
  - add-to-cart
  - wishlist icon
```

## Code quality checklist after Figma implementation

Before finishing:

- The implementation matches the Figma visual intent.
- It uses WordPress templates/template parts, not static HTML pages.
- Editable content is modeled with ACF or CPT fields.
- Product content comes from `selecta_product`, not hardcoded mock cards.
- No WooCommerce or commerce logic was introduced.
- No public auth/account UI was introduced.
- Dynamic output is escaped.
- Images use WordPress image functions where possible.
- CSS is plain and component-scoped.
- The layout is responsive.
- Keyboard/focus behavior is handled.
- ARIA states are correct for interactive components.
- No unnecessary JS library was added.
- The component remains reusable.

## Troubleshooting Figma MCP

If the MCP returns too much or gets slow:

- Use a smaller selected frame.
- Select one component instead of the whole page.
- Ask for design context first, then code after confirming mapping.
- Avoid heavy nested frames.
- Use clear prompts with file targets and project constraints.

If the MCP produces React/web-app style code:

- Do not use it directly.
- Translate the structure into PHP template parts and plain CSS.
- Use WordPress data sources and ACF.
- Remove app-state/auth/e-commerce assumptions.

If Figma variables are available:

- Extract them into CSS custom properties.
- Use the existing CSS token naming convention.
- Avoid creating duplicate token systems.

If variables are not available:

- Infer a minimal token set from repeated colors, spacing, type sizes, and radii.
- Do not over-engineer.

